{
  "response": "# Verdict: REJECT\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The `BuggyExpandable` component is fundamentally inaccessible. It uses a non-interactive `<div>` with an `onClick` handler in place of a native button, completely locking out keyboard-only users and failing to expose any role, state, or controls relationships to assistive technology. It fails the core requirements of the WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern and violates multiple Level A WCAG criteria.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-commitment Predictions\nBefore inspecting the implementation, an expandable/disclosure widget was predicted to exhibit:\n1. **Interactive element anti-pattern**: Non-semantic `<div>` or `<span>` used as trigger without native keyboard activation.\n2. **Missing state communication**: `aria-expanded` missing or desynchronized from visual state.\n3. **Missing relationship association**: Disclosure content lacking an `id` linked to the trigger via `aria-controls`.\n4. **State symbol verbosity**: Text characters (`+`/`\u2212`) announced literally by screen readers (\"plus\", \"minus\") rather than hidden via `aria-hidden=\"true\"`.\n5. **Keyboard trap or reachability failure**: Control skipped entirely in the Tab order.\n\n*All 5 predicted anti-patterns are present in this component.*\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n#### 1. Non-interactive `<div>` trigger locks out keyboard navigation\n- **Location**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:12-19`\n- **Element**: `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle}>`\n- **Impacted Users**: Keyboard-only, switch device, and screen reader users.\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A), WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A), WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure (Show/Hide) Pattern.\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: A `<div>` element is not focusable by default and does not respond to standard keyboard activation keys (`Enter` or `Space`). Keyboard-only users tab straight past the trigger and have zero mechanism to activate it or access the hidden content. Furthermore, screen readers announce this as static text rather than an actionable control.\n- **Fix**: Replace `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\">` with a native `<button type=\"button\">`. Native buttons inherently handle Tab focus, Enter/Space activation, and announce the `button` role without requiring custom event listeners or `tabindex`.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: disclosure-div-trigger-keyboard-lockout\nfingerprint: a1b2c3d4e5f60718\nsource: BuggyExpandable.jsx:12-19\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard, WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value, APG Disclosure Pattern\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 2.1.1, 4.1.2 (Section 508 Chapter 5 - 501.1)\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: keyboard=HIGH, screen_reader=HIGH, low_vision=MEDIUM, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: BuggyExpandable.jsx:12: <div className=\"expandable-trigger\" onClick={toggle}>\nreproduction_steps: 1. Mount BuggyExpandable. 2. Attempt to press Tab to reach the trigger. 3. Observe focus skips the element entirely. 4. Attempt Enter/Space; trigger does not fire.\nexpected_behavior: Trigger receives keyboard focus via Tab and toggles content on Enter and Space keypresses.\nactual_behavior: Element is not focusable and keyboard activation is impossible.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n#### 2. Missing `aria-expanded` programmatic state communication\n- **Location**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:12-19`\n- **Element**: `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\">`\n- **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users (VoiceOver, NVDA, JAWS).\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A), WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern.\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: Screen reader users are given no indication that this control controls an expandable region, nor whether that region is currently collapsed or expanded. Visually, a user sees the state change, but programmatically the state is invisible.\n- **Fix**: Add `aria-expanded={isExpanded}` to the trigger element (which should be a `<button>`).\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: disclosure-missing-aria-expanded\nfingerprint: b2c3d4e5f6a7b8c9\nsource: BuggyExpandable.jsx:12-19\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value, APG Disclosure Pattern\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 4.1.2\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=LOW, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: BuggyExpandable.jsx:12: No aria-expanded attribute present on trigger element.\nreproduction_steps: 1. Inspect accessibility tree in DevTools. 2. Observe element has no expandable/collapsed state attribute.\nexpected_behavior: Element exposes expanded: false when collapsed and expanded: true when open.\nactual_behavior: No state is exposed to the accessibility tree.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n#### 1. Missing programmatic relationship (`aria-controls` / panel ID)\n- **Location**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:21-25`\n- **Element**: `<div className=\"expandable-content\">`\n- **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users.\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A), WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure Pattern.\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: Without an `id` on the disclosure container and a matching `aria-controls` on the trigger, assistive technologies cannot communicate the structural link between the control and the region it governs.\n- **Fix**: Generate a stable or unique ID (e.g., via `useId()`) on the content panel and link it with `aria-controls={contentId}` on the trigger button.\n\n#### 2. Raw text symbol (`+` / `\u2212`) causes redundant screen reader speech\n- **Location**: `BuggyExpandable.jsx:17`\n- **Element**: `{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}`\n- **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users.\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 1.1.1 Non-text Content (Level A), WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: The `+` and `\u2212` symbols are intended as visual indicators of state. When left unshielded inside the accessible name calculation, screen readers announce: *\"[Title] plus, button\"* or *\"[Title] minus, button, expanded\"*. Once `aria-expanded` is introduced, reading the symbol verbally is confusing and redundant.\n- **Fix**: Wrap the toggle symbol in `<span aria-hidden=\"true\">{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}</span>`.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings & Enhancements\n\n#### Enhancements\n- **Heading wrapper for disclosure context**: If this component is used as part of a document section or accordion, the trigger button should be wrapped in an appropriate heading tag (`<h2>`, `<h3>`, etc.) per WAI-ARIA APG Accordion pattern.\n- **Preserve DOM presence with `hidden` vs unmounting**: Currently, `isExpanded && <div className=\"expandable-content\">` conditionally unmounts the DOM node. While permissible for disclosures, using `id={contentId} hidden={!isExpanded}` maintains DOM presence and allows screen reader virtual buffers to track the controlled region reliably.\n\n---\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Missing interactive role, missing `aria-expanded`, missing `aria-controls`, unhidden `+`/`\u2212` symbols |\n| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | `div` with `onClick` is completely omitted from the Tab sequence; zero keyboard operability |\n| Low vision | **MEDIUM** | Missing native focus ring indicator; no contrast guarantees on `:focus-visible` |\n| Cognitive | **MEDIUM** | Inconsistent interactive affordance; screen reader and keyboard behaviors fail expectations |\n| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No animations or auto-playing movement present |\n| Auditory access | **LOW** | No audio or video media elements present |\n| Environmental contrast | **LOW** | Styling handled externally, but semantic markup missing |\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing (Gap Analysis)\n\n1. **Native Interactive Semantics**: `<button type=\"button\">` is absent.\n2. **Dynamic State Binding**: `aria-expanded` is completely absent.\n3. **Control Association**: `aria-controls` pointing to a content container ID is absent.\n4. **Symbol Isolation**: `aria-hidden=\"true\"` on the visual indicator glyphs is absent.\n5. **Keyboard Event Listeners**: If implemented as a custom widget, `onKeyDown` (Space/Enter) would be required; using a native `<button>` provides this out-of-the-box.\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen reader user**: VoiceOver/NVDA reads `expandable-trigger` as plain static text followed by the word \"plus\" or \"minus\". There is no announcement that the element can be activated, nor that content has appeared or collapsed.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Pressing Tab moves focus directly past this component to the next focusable element on the page. The user has no way of knowing content exists under this trigger.\n- **Low vision user**: In Windows High Contrast Mode / Forced Colors, native `<button>` elements receive system-defined borders and focus outlines; a plain `<div>` will not receive button contrast styling, making hit targets ambiguous.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: Predictability is broken. An element visually formatted as a button fails to respond to common interaction models (Tab + Enter/Space).\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Remediation\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useId } from 'react';\n\nconst AccessibleExpandable = ({ title, content }) => {\n  const [isExpanded, setIsExpanded] = useState(false);\n  const contentId = useId();\n\n  const toggle = () => {\n    setIsExpanded((prev) => !prev);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"expandable-section\">\n      <button\n        type=\"button\"\n        className=\"expandable-trigger\"\n        onClick={toggle}\n        aria-expanded={isExpanded}\n        aria-controls={contentId}\n      >\n        <span className=\"expandable-title\">{title}</span>\n        <span className=\"expandable-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n          {isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}\n        </span>\n      </button>\n\n      <div\n        id={contentId}\n        className=\"expandable-content\"\n        hidden={!isExpanded}\n      >\n        {content}\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n};\n\nexport default AccessibleExpandable;\n```\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n\n**Verdict: REJECT**\n- **Reason**: The component violates foundational Level A requirements (WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard, WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value). Keyboard users are blocked from activating the component, and screen readers receive neither a role nor an expansion state.\n- **Criteria to upgrade to ACCEPT**:\n  1. Replace the trigger `<div>` with a `<button type=\"button\">`.\n  2. Implement `aria-expanded={isExpanded}` on the button.\n  3. Associate the button with the content panel using `aria-controls` and `id`.\n  4. Wrap visual glyphs (`+` / `\u2212`) in `aria-hidden=\"true\"`.\n\n### Open Questions (Unscored)\n1. *Document Structure*: Is this expandable intended as part of an accordion list? If so, the `<button>` should be wrapped in an appropriate semantic heading (`<h3>`, etc.) reflecting page hierarchy.\n",
